.TH PASSWD 1
.SH NAME
passwd \- change a password
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B passwd
.RI [ name ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B passwd
changes your own password: it asks for the current one, then the new one
twice (hidden). Root may set anyone's, without the current one. A
password has at least 8 characters, and is not the account's name.
.PP
It only asks the accounts server,
.BR auth (8),
which decides from the caller's identity. The home's key is wrapped by
the new password; when root sets the password of someone whose home is
locked (they have not logged in since the machine started), the home
keeps the old password.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the password changed; 1 otherwise.
.SH FILES
.B /etc/secrets
(through
.BR auth (8)).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR su (1),
.BR useradd (8),
.BR users (5),
.BR auth (8)
